Meend Jangle
Ingredients: K-INDIE + INDIAN RAGA + CELESTIAL, SPACE & ORBITAL + Clap  
Creative Catalyst: The style was codified by grandmothers who DJ at a floating market before dawn.

Meend Jangle fuses the bittersweet, chorus-saturated electric guitars and wistful vocal melodies of South Korean indie pop with the microtonal glides (meend) and meditative modal architecture of classical Indian ragas. Anchored by wide, tactile handclaps rather than traditional trap kits, the sound is suspended inside cavernous tape echoes and celestial synthesizer pads that evoke satellites drifting through pre-dawn river mist.

Genre DNA
 Tempo & Pulse: 68–82 BPM. Rhythmic structures abandon rigid Western grids in favor of supple, asymmetrical talas (often 7-beat rupak or relaxed 16-beat tintal* patterns) marked by crisp, syncopated room claps and the slow bobbing sway of rivercraft.
 Harmonic Palette: Bittersweet Korean indie major-seventh chords layered over resonant root-fifth tanpura drones, frequently shifting into early-morning modes like Raga Bhairav and Raga Todi* with flattened seconds and augmented fourths that produce an aching cosmic yearning.
 Production Signatures: (1) Orbital Alap Processing: Clean electric guitar jangle and sliding sarod notes routed into stereo reel-to-reel tape delays and eventide pitch-shifted pitch blooms; (2) Dry/Wet Percussive Polarity: Tactile, bone-dry handclaps placed dead center to contrast against cavernous, zero-gravity stereo atmospheres; (3) Fluttering Drone Bed:* Microcassette flutter and slight tape warble embedded beneath electronic sruti boxes to mimic early-morning air.
 Dynamics: Tracks commence without meter in a weightless, floating alap* of drifting synthesizer swells and plucked slides, gradually coalescing as a solitary handclap establishes the cycle, building into an ecstatic yet gentle cascade of jangly riffs before floating back into planetary silence.

Scene & Origin Lore
Meend Jangle coalesced along the brackish canal junctions of the Nakdong River delta, where elderly matriarchs (halmonis) gathered on flat-bottom wooden barges before 4:00 AM to vend winter melons, dried anchovies, and medicinal herbs. To keep fellow boaters calm in the freezing navigation fog, several grandmothers rigged dual-well cassette decks to solar batteries, cross-fading worn Hindustani classical tapes left behind by Gujarati cargo crews with gentle 1990s Korean college-rock broadcasts. The resulting aesthetic was quickly adopted by young Busan and Seoul runaways, who embraced the grandmothers' uniform of quilted work vests (jokki), loose indigo linen trousers, and finger-tapped wooden clappers. Early morning pontoon jams became hush-hush communal rituals, with musicians sitting cross-legged on wet cedar skiffs, tuning Telecasters to open drone frequencies as the first shipping freighters cut across the bay.

Typical Instruments: Fender Telecaster (clean, heavily chorused through vintage Roland amplifiers), Sarod, Sitar, Electronic Raagini Tanpura drone boxes, dry acoustic handclaps and wooden kartal clappers, Roland Space Echo RE-201, polyphonic analog pads, soft breathy female and male vocal harmonies.

For Fans Of: Say Sue Me, Floating Points & Pharoah Sanders, Anoushka Shankar, Hyukoh.

